WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.925
&gt;&gt; Let me thank

00:00:05.926 --> 00:00:09.210
all of you for holding
on so long all day.

00:00:09.211 --> 00:00:10.750
This is really amazing,

00:00:10.751 --> 00:00:12.200
the room was full at 9:00

00:00:12.201 --> 00:00:14.625
AM and it's still
pretty full now.

00:00:14.626 --> 00:00:17.955
It's amazing in
this horrible weather.

00:00:17.956 --> 00:00:23.010
I really loved the day because I

00:00:23.011 --> 00:00:28.910
think I know this

00:00:28.911 --> 00:00:31.340
was the panel that was
called the future,

00:00:31.341 --> 00:00:33.530
but I think in essence,

00:00:33.531 --> 00:00:36.135
all of it was about the future.

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:40.120
I think that talking about

00:00:40.121 --> 00:00:45.315
theory as Robert Douglas panel
did about the beauty,

00:00:45.316 --> 00:00:50.375
about these ideas that
we find and we follow,

00:00:50.376 --> 00:00:52.700
and that resonate and give

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:56.450
us new ways of looking
at the world on how they

00:00:56.451 --> 00:00:58.970
pay off on anticipated ways

00:00:58.971 --> 00:01:03.950
as the gems that they are
thinking will always do.

00:01:03.951 --> 00:01:07.670
And then, second panel
on machine learning

00:01:07.671 --> 00:01:10.940
which now are so much hype around

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:13.460
machine learning but there's also

00:01:13.461 --> 00:01:17.570
so much promise and
so many questions.

00:01:17.571 --> 00:01:20.255
I think for us, yes

00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:23.540
there are so many applications
of machine learning

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:26.690
that we have yet to see in

00:01:26.691 --> 00:01:30.155
the healthcare domain and
in so many other domains,

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.950
but I think again we're
going to find that strand of

00:01:33.951 --> 00:01:38.495
beauty we don't yet
understand why it works,

00:01:38.496 --> 00:01:42.920
and I think that will bring those

00:01:42.921 --> 00:01:47.240
of us who love
theory and those of

00:01:47.241 --> 00:01:50.810
us who care deeply about

00:01:50.811 --> 00:01:54.860
the social good that

00:01:54.861 --> 00:01:57.635
can come out of
machine learning together.

00:01:57.636 --> 00:01:59.330
We're really just at

00:01:59.331 --> 00:02:02.065
the very beginning
of machine learning.

00:02:02.066 --> 00:02:04.010
Then, I guess we had

00:02:04.011 --> 00:02:10.105
the panel on platforms
which I found amazing,

00:02:10.106 --> 00:02:14.120
from scholars who really

00:02:14.121 --> 00:02:17.930
study platforms with
their two new books

00:02:17.931 --> 00:02:20.645
which were amazing to

00:02:20.646 --> 00:02:23.600
someone who's the Chief
Economist for one of

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:26.945
the biggest platforms
in the world and

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.720
one that is most functional
due to the fact that it

00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:33.825
came with its own set
of social norms,

00:02:33.826 --> 00:02:37.350
which was really a blessing
for LinkedIn, right?

00:02:37.351 --> 00:02:41.445
The other ones were
the norms, who knows?

00:02:41.446 --> 00:02:43.580
With Bridget on it,

00:02:43.581 --> 00:02:46.440
how many millions of
followers do she have?

00:02:46.441 --> 00:02:49.650
Four point six million followers,

00:02:49.651 --> 00:02:51.645
I mean, just amazing.

00:02:51.646 --> 00:02:56.080
And I love the fact that
she's back in grad school.

00:02:56.081 --> 00:02:58.690
As she creates this following,

00:02:58.691 --> 00:03:00.700
she's studying this following,

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:03.240
and to me that was just amazing.

00:03:03.241 --> 00:03:06.365
Then the Fate panel,

00:03:06.366 --> 00:03:08.310
Hannah's Fate panel, fairness,

00:03:08.311 --> 00:03:10.680
accountability, transparency,

00:03:10.681 --> 00:03:13.360
and ethics, which I

00:03:13.361 --> 00:03:20.130
think is one of the most
important developments

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:27.060
of the past decade that we want

00:03:27.061 --> 00:03:32.610
to find ways to rather

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:39.135
than having AI compound
the injustices,

00:03:39.136 --> 00:03:42.120
make things less interpretable,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.055
rather than doing that,

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:47.155
having AI help us

00:03:47.156 --> 00:03:49.839
ultimately to make decisions

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.150
that are fairer than
human judgments,

00:03:52.151 --> 00:03:55.050
but we must define what
we mean by fairness,

00:03:55.051 --> 00:03:58.899
which is why we must work
with legal scholars,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.310
and ethicists, and philosophers.

00:04:01.311 --> 00:04:04.400
Then finally, this panel
which as you can see,

00:04:04.401 --> 00:04:12.080
I stacked with people who
don't make wild predictions of

00:04:12.081 --> 00:04:14.630
the future but who ground us

00:04:14.631 --> 00:04:21.225
and provide motivations
for that path.

00:04:21.226 --> 00:04:23.760
We should be looking

00:04:23.761 --> 00:04:28.410
towards taking
all these other things into,

00:04:28.411 --> 00:04:31.360
I mean I just love this lab.

00:04:31.361 --> 00:04:38.375
I love MSR in general and really,

00:04:38.376 --> 00:04:43.100
I am deeply indebted to Microsoft

00:04:43.101 --> 00:04:48.665
for supporting us in doing
this kind of research.

00:04:48.666 --> 00:04:53.660
I think because we're pretty
smart so we figure out

00:04:53.661 --> 00:04:55.670
ways to make this pay off for

00:04:55.671 --> 00:04:59.025
the company because we live
in a world of incentives,

00:04:59.026 --> 00:05:03.075
but it's just an
additional constraint,

00:05:03.076 --> 00:05:04.595
we want to do great things,

00:05:04.596 --> 00:05:06.680
create great social good,

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:12.470
and also make
this a good proposition

00:05:12.471 --> 00:05:14.435
so that other companies
will do this.

00:05:14.436 --> 00:05:17.300
As I say, just another
constraint and

00:05:17.301 --> 00:05:20.540
the unconstrained life
is not worth living.

00:05:20.541 --> 00:05:23.180
So, thank you everyone.

00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:30.380
Oh my god,

00:05:30.381 --> 00:05:34.845
and there are two people
I need to thank them.

00:05:34.846 --> 00:05:38.285
Sit down there because
you are one of them.

00:05:38.286 --> 00:05:42.515
Nicole Emerlita and her committee

00:05:42.516 --> 00:05:46.040
put together
this amazing program.

00:05:46.041 --> 00:05:47.180
One thing that's
happened to me as I

00:05:47.181 --> 00:05:48.605
get one more labs to manage,

00:05:48.606 --> 00:05:55.080
one more jobs that
I have to let go,

00:05:55.081 --> 00:05:58.090
and I don't want to let go
the people who are amazing,

00:05:58.091 --> 00:06:00.470
and I let go
the planning of this to

00:06:00.471 --> 00:06:03.080
Nicole who did an amazing job

00:06:03.081 --> 00:06:05.480
and to Lauren who's
back there who

00:06:05.481 --> 00:06:08.855
doesn't like to be called
out but there she is,

00:06:08.856 --> 00:06:13.075
who just did a spectacular job.

00:06:13.076 --> 00:06:17.010
I only trust
brilliant perfectionist

00:06:17.011 --> 00:06:20.020
and both of you are
brilliant perfectionists.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:25.935
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Jennifer.

00:06:25.936 --> 00:06:27.770
It was a great pleasure to share

00:06:27.771 --> 00:06:31.420
this wonderful day with all
of you and I'm quite pleased.

00:06:31.421 --> 00:06:32.840
We could give a round of applause

00:06:32.841 --> 00:06:34.310
to all of our panelists
that I think

00:06:34.311 --> 00:06:38.220
we chose very well right now.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:43.800
There is now a reception
from 5-7 with a live band.

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:47.180
There's going to be
a little surprise around 5:30

00:06:47.181 --> 00:06:51.650
I think so stick
around, it'll be great.

